 <bp:displayName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism</bp:displayName>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Glycine, serine and...</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">serine and threonine metabolism - Homo sapiens (human)</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Glycine</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Serine is derived from 3-phospho-D-glycerate, an intermediate of glycolysis [MD:M00020], and glycine is derived from serine. Threonine is an essential amino acid, which animals cannot synthesize. In bacteria and plants, threonine is derived from aspartate [MD:M00018].</bp:comment>
